<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Padmore</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1903">1903</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Malcolm Ivan Meredith Nurse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Padmore was a Trinidadian Pan-Africanist, journalist and author. A Communist Party member from 1927, he headed the Negro Bureau of the Red International of Labour Unions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and edited The Negro Worker, but broke with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a> in 1933 over what he saw as its flagging support for colonial independence. Settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> from 1934, he chaired the International African Service Bureau and organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-pan-african-congress">Fifth Pan-African Congress</a> at Manchester in 1945. He advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a> and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, where his ashes were buried after his death in London.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-padmore.jpg" alt="George Padmore" width="320" height="518" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Padmore,_circa_1937.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
